{
  "answer": "Because SQL's three-valued logic treats NULL as UNKNOWN in comparisons, so salary <> 100 evaluates to UNKNOWN for NULL values, which are filtered out in the WHERE clause. To include NULLs, you must explicitly use salary <> 100 OR salary IS NULL.",
  "insufficient": false,
  "citations": [
    {
      "sectionId": "null-where",
      "quote": "For salaries 100, 200, and NULL, WHERE salary <> 100 returns only the row with  200. To include missing salaries, write salary <> 100 OR salary IS NULL."
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "null-logic",
      "quote": "In SQL three-valued logic, NOT UNKNOWN is UNKNOWN. TRUE OR UNKNOWN is TRUE, FALSE OR UNKNOWN is UNKNOWN, FALSE AND UNKNOWN is FALSE, and TRUE AND UNKNOWN is UNKNOWN. Thus NOT (salary = 100) still excludes rows with NULL salary in a WHERE clause."
    }
  ]
}

